# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/010_anonymous_struct_union.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 010_anonymous_struct_union
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = Anonymous struct and union members and their name lookup
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
Each anonymous-union member is written and read back through the same member, so no type
punning is relied upon and no padding byte is observed.  Every member is assigned before it
is read, all values stay inside the range of their types, and both aggregate sizes are
printed rather than assumed so they are compared like every other value.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
anon_union_direct=1 16
anon_union_halves=2 5 6
anon_struct_lookup=4 5 6 7
runtime_lookup=14
sizes=8 16
END
